SELECT set_config('request.jwt.claim.role','service_role',false);
SELECT set_config('request.jwt.claim.sub','f49d6153-1835-4455-81f3-2918d5d3484e',false);
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A merchandiser evaluates a ''dynamic pricing'' e-commerce strategy (algorithmically adjusting online prices in real-time based on demand signals, inventory levels, and competitor pricing) against traditional fixed seasonal pricing. What CUSTOMER-TRUST risk does dynamic pricing introduce that fixed pricing avoids, connecting to the earlier markdown-cadence customer-behavior discussion?', '["Dynamic pricing carries no customer-trust risk whatsoever, making this comparison to fixed pricing technically pointless from a customer-relationship perspective","If customers discover that IDENTICAL products show DIFFERENT prices to different customers at the same time (or notice prices fluctuating unpredictably even within a single shopping session), this can damage trust and perceived fairness — similar in principle to the earlier markdown-cadence discussion about customers reacting strategically to perceived pricing patterns, but potentially more severe since dynamic pricing can create a perception of being individually ''targeted'' or treated unfairly compared to other customers, a distinct reputational risk beyond simple strategic purchase-timing delay","Dynamic pricing always increases customer trust and loyalty compared to fixed pricing, the reverse of the actual, well-documented customer-perception risk associated with real-time algorithmic price adjustment","Customer trust has no relationship to pricing strategy or perceived fairness, making this entire concern about dynamic pricing''s customer-relationship risk technically unfounded"]'::jsonb, 'If customers discover that IDENTICAL products show DIFFERENT prices to different customers at the same time (or notice prices fluctuating unpredictably even within a single shopping session), this can damage trust and perceived fairness — similar in principle to the earlier markdown-cadence discussion about customers reacting strategically to perceived pricing patterns, but potentially more severe since dynamic pricing can create a perception of being individually ''targeted'' or treated unfairly compared to other customers, a distinct reputational risk beyond simple strategic purchase-timing delay', 'This extends the earlier markdown-cadence/customer-behavior-learning discussion to a related but distinct risk category — while markdown cadence predictability risks customers strategically DELAYING purchases, dynamic/personalized pricing risks a more severe FAIRNESS/TRUST perception problem: if customers discover (or suspect) that different customers see different prices for the identical product at the same time, this can feel like unfair discriminatory treatment rather than simply a predictable sale pattern, potentially causing more severe brand trust damage and negative publicity than the milder strategic-delay behavior markdown predictability creates — illustrating that not all pricing-strategy customer-behavior risks are equivalent in severity, even though they share the underlying theme of customers reacting to perceived pricing patterns.', 3, '{"dynamic-pricing-trust-risk"}'::text[]),
('8b81a645-306d-4cc1-962a-ab597d640fe5', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A merchandiser calculates ''sell-through velocity'' (units sold per week) for a style during its first 4 weeks: 180 / 165 / 140 / 95 units. Using this DECLINING weekly pattern (not just the total), and knowing the style still has 600 units of remaining inventory, what does this pattern suggest about REORDER decision-making, connecting to the earlier sell-through-pattern-analysis discussion?', '["The declining weekly pattern has no relationship to reorder decision-making; a reorder decision should be based purely on TOTAL units sold so far (580 units), with zero consideration of the declining week-over-week trend","The consistently DECLINING week-over-week velocity (180→165→140→95, roughly a 15-25% decline each week) suggests initial demand may have been front-loaded/novelty-driven (echoing the earlier sell-through-pattern-analysis discussion) and is now naturally tapering — placing a REORDER based on the strong early weeks'' velocity (without accounting for this declining trend) risks over-ordering into a naturally slowing demand curve, and the large remaining inventory (600 units) combined with the declining sell-through rate suggests this style may not need replenishment at all, or need a much smaller reorder than the early weeks'' pace alone would suggest","A declining weekly sell-through pattern always indicates a MANUFACTURING QUALITY DEFECT is causing returns, making any interpretation involving demand-pattern analysis technically incorrect for this specific data pattern","Reorder quantity should always be calculated as exactly equal to the FIRST week''s sales figure (180 units) applied repeatedly, regardless of any observed trend in the subsequent weeks'' data"]'::jsonb, 'The consistently DECLINING week-over-week velocity (180→165→140→95, roughly a 15-25% decline each week) suggests initial demand may have been front-loaded/novelty-driven (echoing the earlier sell-through-pattern-analysis discussion) and is now naturally tapering — placing a REORDER based on the strong early weeks'' velocity (without accounting for this declining trend) risks over-ordering into a naturally slowing demand curve, and the large remaining inventory (600 units) combined with the declining sell-through rate suggests this style may not need replenishment at all, or need a much smaller reorder than the early weeks'' pace alone would suggest', 'This directly applies the earlier sell-through-pattern-analysis principle (velocity SHAPE, not just total, carries strategic information) to a concrete reorder decision — a consistently DECLINING week-over-week pattern (unlike a STEADY pattern) suggests the initial strong weeks may reflect front-loaded/early-adopter demand that''s naturally tapering, rather than a sustained, ongoing demand level — a reorder decision based on the STRONG EARLY weeks'' pace (ignoring this declining trend) risks significantly over-ordering into demand that''s already slowing, and combined with the substantial 600 units of remaining inventory, this pattern suggests caution (a smaller reorder or none at all) rather than assuming the strong early sell-through will continue at anything close to its initial pace.', 3, '{"declining-velocity-reorder-decision"}'::text[]);
COMMIT;
